WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.360
People are finally starting to&nbsp;
wake up to something crucial.&nbsp;

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:09.840
Something I've been talking about for years when&nbsp;
Republicans waged a war against so-called council&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:16.480
culture, when the right decries businesses&nbsp;
making decisions about what's best for them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:22.240
because of backlash from the public, what they&nbsp;
are criticizing, what they are fighting against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:27.200
is the very free market that they have&nbsp;
been defending almost to the death. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:32.480
it's almost a religion for these people, the&nbsp;
free market. And they've been doing it really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:41.040
since the reorganization of Republican politics in&nbsp;
the 1980s, this prayer at the altar of free market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:48.160
capitalism. And this exposes a deeper truth&nbsp;
about the American right wing. Not only are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:55.600
they short on policy, as we've talked about many&nbsp;
times. And not only do they simply play on values,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:02.240
even the values they play on are only relevant&nbsp;
up to the point at which it becomes inconvenient.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:09.520
What I mean by that is they talk about the power&nbsp;
and the efficiency of markets to guide economies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:15.600
and cultures and countries. They say markets&nbsp;
are much smarter than government officials or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:21.440
politicians. But then, as we saw this week, when&nbsp;
Dr. Seuss's publisher decides, you know, here's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:27.040
six books out of a whole bunch, here's six books&nbsp;
that we're going to stop publishing because some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:33.280
of the content doesn't really fit into what are&nbsp;
considered acceptable sort of social norms and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:40.240
portrayals of minorities. Right now. That's a&nbsp;
free market decision made by a for profit company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:44.960
because they looked at public opinion and they&nbsp;
said, let's make the decision that's best for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:50.880
our bottom line. And Republicans instantly&nbsp;
throw their supposed values out the window&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:58.320
and spend three days. And probably it'll&nbsp;
be more talking about the travesty of Dr.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:04.880
Seuss's so-called cancelation. So not only do they&nbsp;
abandon their supposed free market principles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:10.160
as soon as they don't like the outcome&nbsp;
dictated by the market, they also weaponize it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:17.440
into the very outraged porn that allows them&nbsp;
to make money by covering this stuff on TV.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:23.440
It's not new that they abandon their values when&nbsp;
it's inconvenient. This is just the latest way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:29.920
Think back when Democrats are in the White House.&nbsp;
All they talk about is debt and deficit and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:35.520
importance of balancing the budget. George W.&nbsp;
Bush comes into office, among other things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:41.440
give tax cuts to the rich and goes to war a really&nbsp;
expensive war with the wrong country and in so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:49.120
doing, explodes the deficit. And they say nothing.&nbsp;
But then Donald Trump comes into office and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:56.240
cuts taxes for the rich, blows up the deficit&nbsp;
again, and they say nothing at all. And yet not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:02.240
only do they abandon their values when it's not&nbsp;
convenient, they want to inject government into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:08.320
it. When a market guides a decision that&nbsp;
they don't like and look at the trajectory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:14.640
on this Dr. Seuss thing, we are for free markets&nbsp;
and keeping government out. This means you would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:19.200
imagine businesses do what they want as long&nbsp;
as it's not illegal and the government stays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:26.160
out of it. Dr. Seuss Enterprises, which made more&nbsp;
than 30 million dollars last year, decides here's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:33.120
six books we will not continue to publish. They're&nbsp;
not canceling Dr Seuss. They're not burning books.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.120
They're saying here's six books we will allow to&nbsp;
go out of print. But if you want to buy a used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:43.120
copy, you can go ahead. And all of a sudden the&nbsp;
sovereignty of business to do what it wants to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:48.320
goes out the window. But not only that,&nbsp;
Republicans, including people like Jim Jordan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:55.600
the congressmen, demand congressional hearings on&nbsp;
council culture. Why is any of this the business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:02.400
of the federal government? Where is Rand Paul as&nbsp;
a principled libertarian to say there is nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:07.520
for the federal government to do related to this?&nbsp;
We should have no role here whatsoever. Hearings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:14.000
no way in hell. These are private businesses&nbsp;
making decisions however they want to make them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:19.600
They're not breaking any law. So quite simply, the&nbsp;
government should have no involvement whatsoever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:26.480
the market, as they like to say, should be&nbsp;
allowed to work its magic. If this decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:31.920
by Souce Enterprises is wrong, then the&nbsp;
market will take care of it, period.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:37.600
If more people are mad about this decision&nbsp;
than the number that like it and they want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:43.680
exercise their ability to participate in free&nbsp;
market capitalism, they can say, well, I'm not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:49.360
going to buy any Dr. Seuss books anymore. I'll&nbsp;
go buy whatever I don't even know, and then the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:55.120
market will take care of it. This is their view&nbsp;
which they now are abandoning. But the truth is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.120 --> 00:05:00.960
that this has nothing to do with their stated&nbsp;
principles. What they mean by the free market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:08.800
is a market that pushes society and culture and&nbsp;
institutions in the way they want it to be pushed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:15.520
They don't like the way that the market forces are&nbsp;
leaning right now in the direction of sensitivity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:20.480
to racial and ethnic issues. They don't like it.&nbsp;
And that's fundamentally what this is all about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:26.320
in this makes, you know, I've said before,&nbsp;
this movement that only wants to talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:32.560
principles. Principles are a great place to&nbsp;
start. But when you only talk about principles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:39.040
you enter what's called the philosophical&nbsp;
black hole. And the really difficult work is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:46.240
figuring out real world situations. This is&nbsp;
so so one part of the principles movement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:51.120
that's bogus is that they very quickly abandon&nbsp;
their stated principles when the outcome isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:56.720
what they want. But this is the other aspect of&nbsp;
it, that when you only talk about principles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:01.440
you end up with situations like this. They don't&nbsp;
like it. That's what this is all about. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:06.560
then this is also extraordinarily profitable for&nbsp;
many. And that's what I want to talk about next.

